1062 #uuid "9e6999c1-79fe-5006-91df-3c25468791e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180522 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180523 #edb/ref 1061 36453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180523 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "145c40ab-6bce-539c-acfa-6819834eff82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180523 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180536 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180536 #edb/ref 1061 36694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180536 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e61e6895-553c-5528-9f47-4ce31a87b2fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180536 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180537 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180537 #edb/ref 1061 36695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180537 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9ed96a02-bacb-559e-9bf4-ebf7f568ed2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180537 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180549 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180549 #edb/ref 1061 4334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180549 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "402d2fd6-c005-5674-be33-360f1b1762d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180549 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180550 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180550 #edb/ref 1061 4336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180550 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e9871c72-8aa0-5596-948e-55a61792f893" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180550 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180636 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180636 #edb/ref 1061 637169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180636 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a4a7f682-c223-50f1-a3eb-6b1f79aad9b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180636 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180637 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180637 #edb/ref 1061 637170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180637 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "91f76525-9d6e-5ea9-a6a3-b6249f246583" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180637 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180638 #edb/ref 1061 637173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180638 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5f14a9d9-61a5-5432-ad78-2673e2e5e50e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180638 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180660 #edb/ref 1064 "Sometimes, we can use ratios to find quantities that aren't explicitly given in the ratio.\n\nFor instance, suppose that the ratio of boys to girls in the fifth grade is $3: 4.$ If there are $15$ boys, how many students are there in total?\n\nNotice that the \"total number of students\" doesn't appear in the ratio. However, we can find the number of students by adding the total number of boys and the number of girls.\n\nWe start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}3}:{\\color{red}4}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}}{{\\color{red}4}}\n$$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of boys and the denominator represents the number of girls.\n\nWe know that there are $15$ boys, so we want to make a numerator of $15.$ We can do this by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}\\times 5}{{\\color{red}4}\\times 5} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}15}}{{\\color{red}20}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since there are $15$ boys in the fifth grade, there must also be $20$ girls. So the total number of students is \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}15} + {\\color{red}20} = 35\\,.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180660 #edb/ref 1065 2051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180660 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ca3cabae-8ebe-5879-b608-9d0ebdb233fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180660 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180739 #edb/ref 1064 "When dealing with sums or differences of square roots, we can combine any terms with the same algebraic expression under the square root symbol $\\sqrt{\\phantom 0}.$ This is similar to collecting like terms in a linear expression.\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n5\\sqrt{x} - 3\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify our expression by combining the two radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\sqrt{x}-3\\sqrt{x} & = \\\\ (5-3)\\sqrt{x} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{x} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe *cannot* combine terms when the expressions under the radical are different. For example, the expression\n\n$$\n5\\sqrt{x} - 3\\sqrt{y}\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt x$ and $\\sqrt y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180739 #edb/ref 1065 5971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180739 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "deda60a8-4b35-50ac-a71c-d3f884aa8e1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180739 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180749 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the product rule for radicals states that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{a\\cdot b} = \\sqrt{a}\\cdot \\sqrt{b}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are nonnegative.\n\nWe can use the product rule for radicals to simplify some expressions. Let's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180749 #edb/ref 1065 6012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180749 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "30549c94-702e-5e00-8cdc-4a050a34243f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180749 #edb/ref 1067 "The Product Rule for Radicals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180750 #edb/ref 1064 "We can simplify cube root expressions in the same way as with square roots.\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n4\\sqrt[3]x - 2\\sqrt[3] x\n$$\n\nWe can simplify our expression by combining the two radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt[3]{x}-2\\sqrt[3]{x} & = \\\\ (4-2)\\sqrt[3]{x} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt[3]{x} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs with square roots, we *cannot* combine terms when the expressions under the radical are different. For example, the expression\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{x-1} + \\sqrt[3]{x+1}\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt[3]{x-1}$ and $\\sqrt[3]{x+1}.$\n\nFinally, it is not possible to combine square root and cube root terms, even if the numbers under the radical symbol are the same! For example, the expression\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt x + \\sqrt[3] x\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt x$ and $\\sqrt[3] x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180750 #edb/ref 1065 6013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180750 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a4a25921-e2bd-5bf7-99ed-3a8dd852fb70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180750 #edb/ref 1067 "Simplifying Expressions with Cube Roots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180785 #edb/ref 1064 "The range of a function can be thought of as the set of $y$-values given by the function's graph. For that reason, it's often helpful to plot the function when determining its range.\n\nFor example, consider the linear function \n\n$$\nf(x) =3-2x,\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any real number. What is the range of $f(x)$?\n\nSince this is a linear function, we only need two points to plot it. For example:\n\n- When $x=0,$ we have $y=3.$ This gives the point $(0,3).$\n\n- When $x=1,$ we have $y=1.$ This gives the point $(1,1).$\n\nPlotting these two points and connecting them, we obtain the following diagram.\n\n![](images/51/5124e487118191ae40248d4488450d065fe71712f709ac62321d749ac17b0127.png)\n\nNotice that the graph $y=f(x)$ will cover all possible $y$-values, so the range of the function is\n\n$$\n-\\infty <          y <          \\infty.\n$$\n\nUsing interval notation, we can also write the range as $(-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nLet's now look at an example of a linear function with a restricted domain." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180785 #edb/ref 1065 8800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180785 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0a33428f-024c-5783-b5d1-b4b0f342d4a6" 